{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner: S2 start-time reconciliation is done against main 86970224 and is attached. Thirty-four requirements are enumerated across adopted layers 2 and 3, each against its note line. Eight of the ten adopted differences bind S2; A9 is S3's alone, and A5 and A8 bind only as prohibitions, so S2 adds no deletion path and no durable field or schema advance. Three Part B items constrain the implementation even though they need no adoption: full canonical identifiers in trailers, the unified settled word list, and the bounded Git environment for identity reads.\n\nThe source map is verified at that head. The request's cited CLI command boundary at cmd/gs/main.go:107 and checkout Git directories at internal/apphost/config.go:420 are both still exact. The metadata lock at internal/apphost/config.go:346 and the atomic file writer at internal/apphost/atomic_file.go:16 are the record-writing machinery. Reference updating at internal/gitstore/gitstore.go:338, with the empty expected-old-value create form already used at internal/kernel/kernel.go:352, gives the compare-and-swap. Bounded attempt allocation with contention treated as a compare-and-swap failure already exists as prior art in internal/app/resident.go:465-488 with eight attempts, and lane attempts follow it. S1's captured read, its advice and its worktree view fields are what S2 consumes rather than recomputing.\n\nThe reconciliation the request required before implementation is settled. Each eligibility check has three outcomes rather than two. Four of the eight determinate refusals, the unsafe destination, the wrong repository, the unwanted existing branch and the foreign genesis, need no projection at all and still fire when the projection is unreadable. Only selector eligibility and record eligibility degrade, and they degrade to a not-established warning that permits creation rather than to a silent pass. A canonical selector proceeds because the resolver returns it without reading the log, while a display-index selector has nothing to resolve and refuses. Settlement is the commitment status field, which is distinct from the separate stale boolean, and stale sits inside the unsettled word list, so a merely stale lane creates with no warning. A permitted duplicate allocates a fresh attempt and never reuses one, and the confirmation flag is not observable anywhere in the compare-and-swap path.\n\nThree items I will not decide alone, because each changes what S2 can honestly claim.\n\nFirst, the configured checkout root still does not exist in the source. S1 recorded that and derived a deliberately narrow root, which is safe while it only over-protects, but the adopted destination refusal reuses the same root to refuse, where narrow means refusing ordinary destinations that should be allowed.\n\nSecond, a per-checkout hook is not possible. A linked checkout's hooks resolve to the shared common directory, so installing the hook is repository-wide however narrowly the flag is described, which the adopted explicit-flag language does not disclose. Appending one canonical trailer only works if the script resolves the record at run time rather than baking in the record it was given at install.\n\nThird, it is unclear whether S2 owes a lane-reference or record read path back into layer 1. The adopted layer-1 text names lane refs and records as claim sources, S1 shipped trailers only, and the bounded inventory reads only heads and remotes. The stated usable result of finding a lane again after its checkout is gone needs some read, but adding one to the association changes a layer 7 contract S1 has just landed.\n\nPlease rule on these three, or tell me to raise them with Hugh as a revised proposal if any answer would change adopted semantics. I have promised the refile and will implement everything the three questions do not touch, holding only the parts they decide.\n\nSeparately for your awareness: internal/workroom/schema.go now reads workroom-fold at 24 while the adoption note still says 23 in two places. That is harmless for S2 and stale for S3's disclosed cost.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:664f3636e9ce559687018f194f52a2556f08867b, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:dc7f7d11f895c96510d3eafe74a47c5bf0dfcab3 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0169ac61d80658478db25ce500d46d087c06fdde, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:01c4e8b02601b3b35241c2baffd15e16e3e3d09d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:033ceaa3c91aa563b1cc51c1b776c47c90d59242, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:04075a23bf804a9e61f04055021af9a18d6ee9ca and 140 more","to":"a6ac0d54"}}